We have to prove that $\varphi$ is a well-defined map and an isomorphism of compact nilspaces. 

We first define a map $\tilde\varphi:F'\rightarrow \mathrm{X}$ using the inverse limit expression of $\mathrm{X}$, namely $\tilde\varphi: (f_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}\in F'=\prod_{i=1}^\infty F_i' \mapsto (\psi_j(p_j((f_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}))))_{j\in \mathbb{N}}$ where $p_j$ is just the projection to the first $j$ coordinates. We claim that $\tilde \varphi$ factors through $\pi_G$, thus yielding a well-defined map $\varphi:F'/G\rightarrow \mathrm{X}$ such that $\tilde\varphi=\varphi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\pi_G$. To prove this, suppose that $\pi_G((f_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}})=\pi_G((h_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}})$. Then there exists $g = (g_j)_{j\in \mathbb{N}}\in G$ where $g_j\in G_j\subset \Theta(\prod_{i=1}^j F_i',(F_i')_{i\in[j]})$ such that for all $j\in \mathbb{N}$, $g_j(f_1,\ldots,f_j)=(h_1,\ldots,h_j)$. As $\psi_j$ is invariant under the action of $G_j$ the claim follows.

Let us now prove that the well-defined map $\varphi$ is bijective. For injectivity, suppose that $\varphi(\pi_G((f_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}))=\varphi(\pi_G((h_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}))$. Then $\psi_j(f_1,\ldots,f_j)= \psi_j(h_1,\ldots,h_j)$ for every $j\in \mathbb{N}$. Starting with $\psi_1(f_1)= \psi_1(h_1)$, by definition of $\psi_1$ there is $g_1\in G_1$ such that $g_1(f_1)=h_1$. Then, by Remark \ref{rem:lifting-of-consistent-trans} there is $g_2\in G_2$ such that $g_2\mapsto g_1$ and $g_2(f_1,f_2)=(h_1,h_2)$. Continuing this process, we deduce that there exists $g=(g_j)\in \varprojlim G_j$ with $g(f_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}=(h_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}$, whence $\pi_G((f_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}})=\pi_G((h_i)_{i\in \mathbb{N}})$, which proves injectivity. Surjectivity follows from Lemma \ref{lem:strong-surjectivity}. 

By the second part of Remark \ref{rem:lifting-of-consistent-trans} we have that $G=\varprojlim G_i$ is fiber-transitive on $F'$.

Lemma \ref{lem:strong-surjectivity} implies that $\varphi$ is an isomorphism of nilspaces (in the sense that $\varphi$ and $\varphi^{-1}$ send cubes to cubes). To see that $\varphi$ is a homeomorphism, it suffices prove that it is continuous and open. To prove that $\varphi$ is continuous note that by definition of inverse limit and the quotient topology on $F'/G$ it suffices to prove that $\zeta_j\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \varphi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \pi_G$ is continuous for every $j\in \mathbb{N}$. However this map equals $\psi_j\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} p_j$ which is continuous. To prove that $\varphi$ is open note first that the sets of the form $p_j^{-1}(U)$ for any $j\in \mathbb{N}$ and any $U\subset \prod_{i=1}^j F_i'$ form a basis of the topology of $F'$. Thus it is enough to prove that for any such $p_j^{-1}(U)$,  $\varphi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \pi_G(p_j^{-1}(U))$ is open.  By Lemma \ref{lem:strong-surjectivity} (for $n=0$) we have that $\varphi(\pi_G(p_j^{-1}(U))) = \zeta_j^{-1}(\psi_j(U))$. As $\psi_j$ is an open map the result  follows.

Finally, we need to prove that for every $n\in \mathbb{N}$, the cube set $\mathrm{C}^n(F'/G)$ is a closed subset of $(F'/G)^{\db{n}}$. However, as $\varphi^{\db{n}}:(F'/G)^{\db{n}}\rightarrow \mathrm{X}^{\db{n}}$ is a homeomorphism  and $\varphi^{\db{n}}(\mathrm{C}^n(F'/G))=\mathrm{C}^n(\mathrm{X})$, the closure of $\mathrm{C}^n(F'/G)$ follows from that of $\mathrm{C}^n(\mathrm{X})$.

