Now let $f\in F'$ and for any $j\in \mathbb{N}$ let $g^{(j-1)}\in \Theta(F_{j-1},(F_i')_{i=1}^{j-1})$ be such that $\psi'_{j-1}(g)=p_j(f)$. We claim that there exists  $g\in \Theta(F',(F_i')_{i\in \mathbb{N}})$ such that $\psi'(g)=f$ and $p_j'(g)=g^{(j-1)}$. To see this, note that by induction it suffices to lift $g^{(j-1)}$ to an element in $\Theta(F_{j},(F_i')_{i=1}^{j})$ with the desired properties. In fact, since we are considering the graded translations, it is clear that we can find such an element. That is, any element of $F_{j}=(F_i')_{i=1}^{j}$ is of the form $(f',f^{(j-1)})\in F_j'\times F_{j-1}$ and we know that $(g^{(j-1)})^{-1}(f_0)=f^{(j-1)}$. The translation $g^{(j)}\in \Theta(F_j,(F_i')_{i=1}^j)$ defined as $(a,b)\in F_j'\times F_{j-1}\mapsto(a+f_0-f',g^{(j-1)}(b))$ works. 

The map $\psi'$ factorizes as $\psi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \pi_K$, where $\pi_K:G\rightarrow K\backslash G$ is the quotient map to the space of right cosets (recall that $G=\Theta(F',(F_i')_{i\in \mathbb{N}})$). To prove that $\psi$ is a homeomorphism it suffices to check that $\psi'$ is open and continuous. Continuity follows from the fact that by definition of $F'$ it suffices to prove that $p_j\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \psi'$ is continuous for every $j\in \mathbb{N}$. Since this equals $\psi_j'\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} p_j'$, which is clearly continuous, the continuity of $\psi'$ follows. To prove openness, note that it is enough to prove it for the basis of the topology on $G$ consisting of sets of the form ${p_j'}^{-1}(U)$ where $U\subset \Theta(F_j,(F_i')_{i=1}^j)$ is open and $j\in \mathbb{N}$. Arguing just as in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:cpct-as-quotient-of-infinite-free}, by the previous paragraph we have that $\psi'({p_j'}^{-1}(U)) = p_j^{-1}(\psi_j(U))$, and the openness follows.

\begin{equation}\label{eq:inf-free-as-coset}
